WEBVTT

00:00:00.262 --> 00:00:02.318
Last video ended with two curves.

00:00:02.681 --> 00:00:05.036
Both follow x squared, up to x equals

00:00:05.198 --> 00:00:05.338
one.

00:00:05.883 --> 00:00:08.230
The first carries on along its tangent, y

00:00:08.332 --> 00:00:10.619
equals two x minus one, and the second

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.124
along y equals x.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.102
Let's turn them into skate tracks.

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:16.672
Roll a skateboard down the first, and it

00:00:16.713 --> 00:00:18.256
passes the join without a bump.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.391
On the second, it jolts, because the track's

00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:22.999
angle changes all at once.

00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:25.751
Both tracks are unbroken, with no gaps.

00:00:26.394 --> 00:00:28.162
Now zoom in on each join.

00:00:28.746 --> 00:00:31.224
The first straightens out into a single line.

00:00:31.748 --> 00:00:34.255
The second stays a corner, however far you

00:00:34.276 --> 00:00:34.516
zoom.

00:00:35.258 --> 00:00:37.389
So what does a join need, for the

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:38.816
curve to have a gradient there?

00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:40.382
Here's the test.

00:00:40.949 --> 00:00:43.476
Take the point P, at x equals a,

00:00:44.039 --> 00:00:46.446
and a nearby point Q, a small step

00:00:46.768 --> 00:00:47.289
h along.

00:00:47.952 --> 00:00:49.717
The gradient of the line through them

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.688
is f of a plus h, minus f

00:00:52.709 --> 00:00:53.892
of a, all over h.

00:00:54.478 --> 00:00:55.250
You met that in Year 11.

00:00:56.096 --> 00:00:59.281
Now shrink h towards zero, from the right,

00:00:59.643 --> 00:01:01.906
and also from the left, because h can

00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:02.367
be negative.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:06.159
If those gradients settle on one finite number,

00:01:06.461 --> 00:01:07.283
from both sides,

00:01:07.886 --> 00:01:09.550
f is differentiable at a.

00:01:10.113 --> 00:01:12.581
That number is f prime of a, and

00:01:12.602 --> 00:01:14.949
the curve has a single tangent there that

00:01:15.111 --> 00:01:15.752
isn't vertical.

00:01:16.496 --> 00:01:18.624
If they don't settle, there's no derivative at

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:18.685
a.

00:01:19.296 --> 00:01:21.348
Try it on the first join, at x

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:21.932
equals one.

00:01:22.576 --> 00:01:24.499
From the right, Q is on the line

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:24.539
y

00:01:25.302 --> 00:01:28.066
equals two x minus one, so every secant

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.588
gradient is exactly two.

00:01:30.431 --> 00:01:32.856
From the left, Q is on x squared,

00:01:33.218 --> 00:01:34.901
and the gradient is two plus h.

00:01:35.403 --> 00:01:37.948
So, one point five, one point nine, one

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:39.773
point nine nine, heading to two.

00:01:40.516 --> 00:01:42.203
Both sides settle on two.

00:01:42.928 --> 00:01:45.458
So this function is differentiable at one, with

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.226
f prime of one equal to two.

00:01:47.949 --> 00:01:50.154
The line is the tangent to x squared,

00:01:50.636 --> 00:01:52.519
so both pieces leave the join in the

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.281
same direction.

00:01:53.843 --> 00:01:55.451
That's why the board doesn't jolt.

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:58.118
Now the second join, where the right piece

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.224
is y equals x.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.465
From the left, nothing changes.

00:02:01.851 --> 00:02:03.300
The gradients still head to two.

00:02:03.905 --> 00:02:06.255
But from the right, every secant gradient is

00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:06.919
exactly one.

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.001
Two from one side, one from the other.

00:02:10.587 --> 00:02:11.731
There's no single number,

00:02:12.355 --> 00:02:14.564
so there's no derivative at x equals one.

00:02:15.449 --> 00:02:18.285
The curve is unbroken, but it isn't differentiable

00:02:18.327 --> 00:02:18.507
there.

00:02:19.413 --> 00:02:20.216
That's what a corner is:

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.809
two different one-sided gradients.

00:02:23.613 --> 00:02:24.916
On the track, that's the jolt,

00:02:25.438 --> 00:02:28.104
from forty-five degrees to about sixty-three, in an

00:02:28.245 --> 00:02:28.585
instant.

00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:31.487
What if the gradients match, but the pieces

00:02:31.588 --> 00:02:32.270
don't meet?

00:02:33.074 --> 00:02:35.207
Here, x squared runs up to one, one.

00:02:36.374 --> 00:02:38.483
Then the right piece is y equals two

00:02:38.664 --> 00:02:40.391
x, which starts at height two.

00:02:41.196 --> 00:02:42.609
On a track, that's a step.

00:02:43.436 --> 00:02:45.487
Its gradient is two, the same as x

00:02:45.507 --> 00:02:46.332
squared at the join.

00:02:47.297 --> 00:02:50.121
A function is continuous at a when, from

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:52.486
both sides, the curve heads to the value

00:02:52.727 --> 00:02:53.728
f of a itself.

00:02:54.672 --> 00:02:56.760
Here, from the right it heads to two,

00:02:57.263 --> 00:02:58.427
but f of one is one.

00:02:59.493 --> 00:03:00.657
So it isn't continuous.

00:03:01.742 --> 00:03:03.707
Now look at the secant from P, to

00:03:03.748 --> 00:03:04.850
a point on the right piece.

00:03:05.613 --> 00:03:08.086
The rise never drops below one, while the

00:03:08.127 --> 00:03:09.071
run shrinks to zero.

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:12.419
So the gradient blows up, to twelve, then

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:13.843
a hundred and two, and beyond.

00:03:14.647 --> 00:03:15.271
No derivative.

00:03:16.096 --> 00:03:18.220
This is why a function that's differentiable at

00:03:18.241 --> 00:03:20.165
a point must be continuous there.

00:03:20.968 --> 00:03:22.935
If the gradients settle on a finite number,

00:03:23.337 --> 00:03:25.646
the rise is that gradient times the run.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:28.213
So the rise shrinks to zero with the

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:29.918
run, and the curve can't jump.

00:03:30.788 --> 00:03:32.974
There's one more way to fail, even with

00:03:33.115 --> 00:03:33.836
no join at all.

00:03:34.479 --> 00:03:36.946
Take y equals the cube root of x,

00:03:37.649 --> 00:03:38.190
at zero.

00:03:38.432 --> 00:03:40.447
It's unbroken, and it looks smooth.

00:03:41.012 --> 00:03:42.956
But the secant gradients are h to the

00:03:42.997 --> 00:03:44.721
power minus two thirds.

00:03:45.524 --> 00:03:47.909
They grow without limit from both sides, because

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.194
the tangent at the origin is vertical.

00:03:50.857 --> 00:03:52.763
A board there would point straight up.

00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:56.472
A vertical tangent has no gradient, so the

00:03:56.513 --> 00:03:59.519
cube root of x isn't differentiable at zero.

00:04:00.382 --> 00:04:02.586
That's why the definition says a tangent that

00:04:02.788 --> 00:04:03.529
isn't vertical.

00:04:04.593 --> 00:04:06.679
At a cusp, a sharp point where both

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.690
sides arrive steeply, the gradients blow up in

00:04:09.852 --> 00:04:10.654
opposite directions.

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:12.904
So there's no derivative there either.

00:04:13.576 --> 00:04:16.312
So at a join, check two things, in

00:04:16.393 --> 00:04:16.573
order.

00:04:17.218 --> 00:04:19.005
First, do the pieces meet?

00:04:19.568 --> 00:04:21.518
Compare the value at the join with the

00:04:21.599 --> 00:04:23.026
height the other piece heads to.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:24.334
If they don't meet,

00:04:24.838 --> 00:04:25.099
stop.

00:04:25.463 --> 00:04:26.649
It's not differentiable.

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:29.161
Second, do the gradients match?

00:04:29.865 --> 00:04:31.668
Each piece here is smooth on its own

00:04:31.769 --> 00:04:35.256
side, so differentiate each one, and evaluate both

00:04:35.377 --> 00:04:35.917
at the join.

00:04:36.682 --> 00:04:38.476
Equal gradients mean differentiable.

00:04:39.001 --> 00:04:40.718
Different gradients mean a corner.

00:04:41.384 --> 00:04:43.111
Continuity on its own isn't enough.

00:04:43.743 --> 00:04:44.999
Now a worked example.

00:04:45.783 --> 00:04:47.810
h of x is x squared for x

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:48.713
up to two,

00:04:49.235 --> 00:04:51.241
and a x plus b for x greater

00:04:51.281 --> 00:04:51.562
than two.

00:04:52.284 --> 00:04:54.149
Find a and b so that h is

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.753
differentiable at two.

00:04:56.739 --> 00:04:57.771
Match the gradients first.

00:04:58.538 --> 00:05:00.768
x squared has gradient two x, which is

00:05:00.889 --> 00:05:02.295
four at x equals two.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.428
The line's gradient is a.

00:05:04.911 --> 00:05:06.158
So a equals four.

00:05:07.126 --> 00:05:08.375
Now make the pieces meet.

00:05:09.222 --> 00:05:10.225
At x equals two, x

00:05:10.809 --> 00:05:12.997
squared is four, and the line gives two

00:05:13.099 --> 00:05:13.740
a plus b.

00:05:14.223 --> 00:05:16.188
So four equals eight plus b,

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.093
and b is minus four.

00:05:18.995 --> 00:05:21.338
The line is y equals four x minus

00:05:21.399 --> 00:05:23.842
four, the tangent to x squared at two,

00:05:24.243 --> 00:05:26.206
so a board rolls through without a jolt.

00:05:27.012 --> 00:05:28.163
You need both equations.

00:05:28.648 --> 00:05:31.095
The gradient alone leaves b free, and the

00:05:31.136 --> 00:05:32.258
join could still jump.

00:05:32.935 --> 00:05:33.938
A short way to hold it:

00:05:34.562 --> 00:05:35.706
meet, then match.

00:05:36.288 --> 00:05:38.811
The pieces meet, so there's no step, and

00:05:38.832 --> 00:05:39.912
the function is continuous.

00:05:40.434 --> 00:05:43.504
The gradients match, so there's no kink, and

00:05:43.666 --> 00:05:44.468
it's differentiable.

00:05:45.154 --> 00:05:46.728
Meeting alone isn't enough.

00:05:49.506 --> 00:05:50.016
Your turn.

00:05:50.581 --> 00:05:53.384
g of x is three minus x squared,

00:05:53.604 --> 00:05:55.606
for x up to and including one,

00:05:56.267 --> 00:05:58.909
and x plus one, for x greater than

00:05:59.049 --> 00:05:59.150
one.

00:05:59.933 --> 00:06:02.173
Is g continuous at x equals one?

00:06:02.817 --> 00:06:03.863
Is it differentiable there?

00:06:04.447 --> 00:06:05.653
Justify both answers.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:07.309
Pause here to answer.

00:06:18.581 --> 00:06:20.465
x equals one belongs to the first piece,

00:06:20.866 --> 00:06:22.931
so g of one is three minus one,

00:06:22.951 --> 00:06:23.552
which is two.

00:06:24.435 --> 00:06:26.589
The second piece heads to one plus one,

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:27.373
also two.

00:06:28.078 --> 00:06:29.986
They meet, so g is continuous.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.583
The gradients are minus two x, which is

00:06:33.764 --> 00:06:34.910
minus two, and one.

00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:39.211
Minus two isn't one, so g isn't differentiable

00:06:39.373 --> 00:06:39.774
at one.

00:06:40.519 --> 00:06:41.990
There's a corner, at one, two.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.297
Being continuous doesn't make it differentiable.

00:06:46.881 --> 00:06:47.324
Last one.

00:06:48.391 --> 00:06:50.095
f of x is e to the two

00:06:50.276 --> 00:06:52.962
x, for x up to and including zero,

00:06:53.583 --> 00:06:56.169
and a x plus b, for x greater

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:56.691
than zero.

00:06:57.453 --> 00:06:59.739
Find the values of a and b that

00:06:59.779 --> 00:07:02.447
make f differentiable at x equals zero.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:03.977
Pause here to answer.

00:07:14.703 --> 00:07:15.709
Match the gradients.

00:07:16.373 --> 00:07:18.120
The derivative of e to the two x

00:07:18.321 --> 00:07:20.390
is two e to the two x, which

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:21.454
is two at zero.

00:07:21.956 --> 00:07:23.664
So a equals two.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.077
Now meet.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:27.083
e to the zero is one,

00:07:27.644 --> 00:07:30.070
and the line gives b at zero, so

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:31.032
b equals one.

00:07:31.755 --> 00:07:33.973
The line is y equals two x plus

00:07:34.095 --> 00:07:34.235
one.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.086
Not b equals zero.

00:07:36.608 --> 00:07:38.415
Then the pieces would miss by one, and

00:07:38.435 --> 00:07:39.339
the curve would jump.

00:07:41.042 --> 00:07:42.128
Back to the smooth join.

00:07:42.772 --> 00:07:45.389
At x equals one, both sides have gradient

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:45.732
two.

00:07:46.235 --> 00:07:48.091
But look at how the gradient changes.

00:07:48.716 --> 00:07:51.178
On the left, it's minus one, then zero,

00:07:51.198 --> 00:07:53.820
then one, still rising as we move right.

00:07:54.622 --> 00:07:56.452
On the right, it stays at two.

00:07:57.055 --> 00:07:59.163
The curve bends, and the line doesn't.

00:08:00.048 --> 00:08:02.695
Next: what the changing gradient tells us about

00:08:02.716 --> 00:08:03.718
the shape of a curve.

00:08:06.555 --> 00:08:07.438
Here are the key notes.

00:08:07.961 --> 00:08:09.729
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:10.231
to copy them.
